---
/**
 * Content rewrite 2026-08 from owner-supplied project facts. Structure:
 * standard tier — Overview / Problem / 2 decision blocks / Outcome. All
 * survey figures removed (owner verdict: unsourceable); page carries no
 * StatsList by design.
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Avito — first job case study · Jury Alyavdin"
  description="A time-boxed hiring exercise for Avito: job search rebuilt around first-time applicants. It advanced me through the panel; the riskiest call drew the hardest pushback."
  path="/casestudy/avito.html"
  h1="Designing Avito's job search around first-time applicants"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/avito/avito-logo.png"
  orbitTop="Avito"
  orbitBottom="C2C Marketplace"
  glow="#f8f8f8"
  heroImage="/casestudy/avito/avito-hero.png"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'iOS' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Product Designer' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2019' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      In 2019, Avito set me a time-boxed design exercise as part of its hiring process: improve how
      people find work through the app. I worked solo — every decision on this page is mine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Avito led the job-search category in Russia, but I chose not to design for everyone. I bet the
      concept on one group: people applying for a job for the first time. The exercise advanced me
      through the interview process without ending in an offer — and the decision the panel pushed
      back on hardest is the one this page is about.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Problem">
    <p>
      Entry-level listings all look the same — similar pay, similar requirements, nothing to tell
      them apart. The jobs behind them lead to very different careers, and the people choosing have
      the least experience to choose with.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Started too broad:</strong> my first pass was a general job-search
      redesign. It had no edge, so I threw it away and narrowed to the wedge where the product could
      matter most — 18-to-25-year-olds after a first job. That segment was my bet, not a research
      finding; the exercise had no field research in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2019, blue-collar hiring in this market still ran offline — phone calls, word of mouth,
      printed notices. The app had to win people who were not looking online at all. To find where
      momentum died, I mapped the journey myself — my own analysis, with no fieldwork behind it:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-cjm.jpg"
      alt="Customer journey map of a first-time applicant, from deciding to work to accepting an offer"
      width={3167}
      height={1262}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="A friend's CV beats a blank template">
    <p>
      The concept starts where a first application starts: with a CV that does not exist yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Rejected: a template library.</strong> Templates are the standard
      answer, and they fail exactly this group — a template is abstract, and someone who has never
      written a CV does not know what belongs in the blanks. What that person does have is a contact
      list full of peers in the same situation. So the concept lets an applicant copy a friend's CV
      and edit it into their own: a real example instead of an empty form.
    </p>
    <p>
      The prototype turns the cold start into an edit — a working draft exists seconds after picking
      a friend:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-contacts"
      alt="Prototype flow of choosing a friend from contacts and copying their CV as a draft"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
    <p>
      The point of copying is confidence: the applicant starts from something that already looks
      plausible.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Employer reviews — the bet the panel called naive">
    <p>
      Before applying, an applicant has to judge the employer with almost nothing to go on. Reviews
      from people who had interviewed or worked there would answer that directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Rejected: protecting the paying side.</strong> Employers are Avito's
      paying customers, and the safe version of this concept leaves them unrated. I argued for
      shipping reviews anyway: evaluating employers was where applicants' effort went, and review
      signal was the direct fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      The resulting screen puts the judgment where the effort was going — review signal on the
      vacancy itself:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-reviews"
      alt="Prototype of employer ratings and review comments attached to a vacancy page"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
    <p>Contacts and employer reviews sit on the jobs category screen itself, above and below the listings:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-advice-contact.png"
      alt="Three screens: category view with contacts and employer reviews, listings, and a vacancy card"
      width={2160}
      height={1440}
    />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>
      Around the two big calls sat quieter scaffolding: contextual tips staged along the funnel,
      expected employer response times, and saved searches. In the prototype, the tips stay inside
      the funnel — help appears at the step it belongs to, not in a separate section:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-advice"
      alt="Prototype of contextual tips appearing along the application funnel stages"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>The vacancy card shows the employer's typical time-to-hire next to the contact buttons:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-duration"
      alt="Flow from the jobs category to a vacancy card showing an average time-to-hire"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>And the feed learns what the applicant is after, so the daily list stops being the same undifferentiated wall:</p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/avito/avito-personalization"
      alt="Personalized jobs-of-the-day feed with a for-you tab above the vacancy list"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>The wireframe flow is where those pieces were sequenced — search, category, listing, vacancy, and the tips and reviews hanging off them:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/avito/avito-wireframe.png" aria-label="Open the full-size wireframe flow">
      <ContentMedia
        src="/casestudy/avito/avito-wireframe.png"
        alt="Annotated wireframe flow linking search, category, listing and vacancy screens"
        width={2727}
        height={1813}
      />
          <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Outcome">
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Advanced through the panel, no offer.</strong> Nothing here was built —
      it was an interview exercise, and it makes no claim on anything Avito shipped afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sharpest feedback landed exactly where I had taken the risk: the panel called employer
      reviews commercially naive. The decision, the pushback, and the outcome are one story — I took
      a risk on the applicants' side of a two-sided market, and the panel priced that risk
      differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Trade-off: the wedge excludes the experienced.</strong> Optimizing the
      funnel for 18-to-25 first-timers leaves experienced blue-collar workers with a worse
      experience.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>
